\section{Input layout}{
Each element of \code{input_files} must be a \emph{wide} table: one row per precursor and
one column per sample. Long-format search engine output (for example a DIA-NN
\code{report.tsv}) must be pivoted to that shape first.

Fractions may carry different ancillary annotation columns; they are combined by
taking the union, with \code{NA} where a fraction lacks a column. Only
\code{precursor_id_column}, \code{protein_aggregator_column} and the abundance columns must
be common to every fraction.
}

\section{Missingness classification}{
When \code{impute_method = "mixed"}, each precursor is tested with a logistic regression
asking whether its \emph{detection} pattern is explained by \code{mnar_variables}. The test
is a likelihood-ratio comparison of the covariate model against an intercept-only
model, with a single Benjamini-Hochberg correction across features.

This detects association between detection and the metadata you supplied. It is a
practical heuristic for routing features to left-censored (QRILC) imputation, not a
proof of the formal missing-not-at-random mechanism, which depends on values that
were never observed.
}

\section{Optional dependencies}{
Some imputation methods are implemented by packages that \code{MsCoreUtils} only
suggests, so they are not installed automatically:
\code{"mixed"} needs missForest and imputeLCMD; \code{"RF"} needs missForest;
\code{"QRILC"}, \code{"MinDet"} and \code{"MinProb"} need imputeLCMD; \code{"knn"} needs impute;
\code{"bpca"} needs pcaMethods; \code{"MLE"} needs norm. Quantile normalisation needs
